An insight is at level L if the person/agent who must learn it is at L:
- Task: only the agent in that task can act on it; if the same task were re-run with a different agent, the lesson would still be "change this brief/file". Its subject is a one-off artifact.
- Project: any agent working in this repo could hit the same condition, and the fix is in this repo's code/tools/conventions. The subject is a repo artifact.
- Agent-type: any agent of that kind, in any project, could hit it, because it is a habit/capability/limit of the agent. The fix is in the agent's instructions or the harness.

The problem: many insights have both. E.g. F-0f2fa841: the specific planted break (task) and the testing rule (agent-type) and the project's export test (project). This is the core finding: an insight records a *failure instance*, and level is a property of the *lesson*, and one instance often yields more than one lesson. The `Source:` field ("where the fix goes") collapses this: it records the fix, not the level.

